e:\n\n\\[\n\\angle I G L = 90^\\circ\n\\]\n\n$I L$ is the diameter of the circumcircle of $\\triangle B G I$. This implies:\n\n\\[\n\\angle I B L = 90^\\circ\n\\]\n\nTherefore, observe that $C K \\parallel G L$ and also $B L \\parallel F K$.\n\n3. Let the lines $C K$ and $B L$ intersect at point $D$. From our previous observations, we note that $D K A L$ forms a parallelogram. This is because in a parallelogram, opposite sides are parallel and equal in length. Here, since:\n\n\\[\nC K \\parallel G L \\quad \\text{and} \\quad B L \\parallel F K\n\\]\n\nWe conclude that $D K A L$ is indeed a parallelogram.\n\n4. Additionally, note that $D$ is the excenter with respect to vertex $A$ of $\\triangle A B C$. This is due to the fact the lines $B L$ and $C K$ are perpendicular to the corresponding internal angle bisectors. \n\n5. Since the excenter $D$ lies on the internal angle bisector $A I$, we conclude that $A I$ must bisect the segment $K L$. Specifically, in a parallelogram, the diagonals bisect each other.\n\nTherefore, since $D K A L$ is a parallelogram and $A I$ is one of the diagonals, it bisects the other diagonal $K L$.\n\n### Conclusion:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{A} \\, I \\, \\text{bisects} \\, K L}\n\\]\n\nThis completes the proof.", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Prove that for \\( 0 \\leq m \\leq n \\):\n\n$$\n\\sum_{k=0}^{n}\\binom{n}{k}\\binom{k}{m}=\\binom{n}{m} 2^{n-m}\n$$", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to prove that the sum from k=0 to n of binomial(n, k) times binomial(k, m) is equal to binomial(n, m) multiplied by 2^(n\u2212m). Hmm, let me start by understanding what each part of this equation represents.\n\nFirst, the left side is a sum over k of two binomial coefficients. The term binomial(n, k) is the number of ways to choose k elements from a set of n elements. Then, binomial(k, m) is the number of ways to choose m elements from those k elements. So, when we multiply them 