lume and Radii**:\n   - The inradius \\(r\\) is given by \\(r = \\frac{3V}{S_{\\text{total}}}\\), where \\(S_{\\text{total}} = S_{\\text{base}} + 3S\\).\n   - The exradius \\(R\\) (radius of the sphere tangent to the base and extensions of the lateral sides) is given by \\(R = \\frac{3V}{S_{\\text{base}} - 3S}\\).\n\n4. **Ratio Calculation**:\n   - Using the relationship for \\(S_{\\text{base}}\\), we substitute into the expressions for \\(r\\) and \\(R\\).\n   - The ratio \\(\\frac{r}{R}\\) simplifies to \\(\\frac{3S - S_{\\text{base}}}{3S + S_{\\text{base}}}\\).\n\n5. **Final Simplification**:\n   - Substituting \\(S_{\\text{base}} = S (\\cos \\alpha + \\cos \\beta + \\cos \\gamma)\\) into the ratio, we get:\n     \\[\n     \\frac{r}{R} = \\frac{3S - S (\\cos \\alpha + \\cos \\beta + \\cos \\gamma)}{3S + S (\\cos \\alpha + \\cos \\beta + \\cos \\gamma)} = \\frac{3 - (\\cos \\alpha + \\cos \\beta + \\cos \\gamma)}{3 + (\\cos \\alpha + \\cos \\beta + \\cos \\gamma)}\n     \\]\n\nThus, the ratio of the radius of the inscribed sphere to the radius of the exsphere is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\dfrac{3 - \\cos \\alpha - \\cos \\beta - \\cos \\gamma}{3 + \\cos \\alpha + \\cos \\beta + \\cos \\gamma}}\n\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "### Problem:\nThe lateral faces of a triangular pyramid are equal in size and form angles $\\alpha, \\beta,$ and $\\gamma$ with the base. Find the ratio of the radius of the sphere inscribed in this pyramid to the radius of the sphere touching the base of the pyramid and the extensions of its lateral edges.\n\n1. Let \\( r \\) and \\( r' \\) be the radii of the inscribed and escribed spheres, respectively.\n2. Let \\( S \\) be the area of one of the lateral faces of the pyramid, and \\( s \\) be the area of the base of the pyramid. Let \\( V \\) be the volume of the pyramid.\n3. The volume \\( V \\) of a pyramid can be expressed in terms of the inscribed radius \\( r \\) as:\n    \\[\n    V = \\frac{(3S + s)r}{3}\n    \\]\n   This comes from the relationship with the inradius, where the total surface area multiplied by the inradius and divided by 3 gives the volume.\n\n4. Similarly, the volume \\( V \\) can be expressed in terms of the escribed radius \\( r' \\) as:\n    \\[\n    V = \\frac{(3S - s)r'}{3}\n    \\]\n   This derives from analogous reasoning as the inscribed sphere, but considers the escribed sphere.\n\n5. From these two relationships, we can equate the volumes:\n    \\[\n    \\frac{(3S + s)r}{3} = \\frac{(3S - s)r'}{3}\n    \\]\n\n6. Solving for the ratio of \\( r \\) to \\( r' \\):\n    \\[\n    (3S + s)r = (3S - s)r'\n    \\]\n    \\[\n    \\frac{r}{r'} = \\frac{3S - s}{3S + s}\n    \\]\n\n7. It is given that the relationship between the base area's cosine angles and the lateral face area is:\n    \\[\n    s = S (\\cos \\alpha + \\cos \\beta + \\cos \\gamma)\n    \\]\n   This is a result referencing problem 2.13.\n\n8. Substituting \\( s \\) with the expression given:\n    \\[\n    \\frac{r}{r'} = \\frac{3S - S(\\cos \\alpha + \\cos \\beta + \\cos \\gamma)}{3S + S(\\cos \\alpha + \\cos \\beta + \\cos \\gamma)}\n    \\]\n\n9. Simplifying the expressions:\n    \\[\n    \\frac{r}{r'} = \\frac{3 - (\\cos \\alpha + \\cos \\beta + \\cos \\gamma)}{3 + (\\cos \\alpha + \\cos \\beta + \\cos \\gamma)}\n    \\]\n\n10. Thus, the ratio is:\n    \\[\n    \\frac{r}{r'} = \\frac{3 - \\cos \\alpha - \\cos \\beta - \\cos \\gamma}{3 + \\cos \\alpha + \\cos \\beta + \\cos \\gamma}\n    \\]\n\n### Conclusion:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{3 - \\cos \\alpha - \\cos \\beta - \\cos \\gamma}{3 + \\cos \\alpha + \\cos \\beta + \\cos \\gamma}}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "As is well known, Robinson Crusoe's friend and companion was named Friday because they met on a Friday. This event occurred in January, but the exact year is uncertain. Robinson is sure, however, that it could have happened with equal probability in any year from 1668 to 1671. Robinson 