ut it's also divisible by 100 (1680 = 16*100 + 80), but 1680 is not divisible by 400. Wait, no, 1680 divided by 100 is 16.8, so it's not a whole number. Wait, actually, 1680 is 16 centuries and 80 years, so 1680 is 1680, which is divisible by 4, 100, but 1680/100=16.8, which is not an integer. Wait, actually, 1680 is 1680, which is 16*100 + 80. Therefore, the year 1680 is not a century year (i.e., not a multiple of 100), so the exception doesn't apply. Therefore, 1680 is a leap year. Wait, but 1680 is divisible by 4 and not a century year, so it is a leap year. Therefore, 1680 is a leap year.\n\nWait, but the range is 1670 to 1680 inclusive. So 1680 is included. Therefore, leap years in 1670-1680 are 1672, 1676, 1680. So 3 leap years.\n\nTherefore, total shift =11 +3=14. 14 mod7=0. So Friday +0=Friday.\n\nWait, but hold on. Let me verify again:\n\nStarting year Y=1670. The years to consider are 1670, 1671,...,1680.\n\n1670: common\n\n1671: common\n\n1672: leap\n\n1673: common\n\n1674: common\n\n1675: common\n\n1676: leap\n\n1677: common\n\n1678: common\n\n1679: common\n\n1680: leap (as established)\n\nTherefore, 1672, 1676, 1680: 3 leap years. So total shift=11+3=14. 14 mod7=0. So the day is Friday.\n\nStarting Year Y=1671:\n\nYears from 1671 to 1681 inclusive.\n\n1671: common\n\n1672: leap\n\n1673: common\n\n1674: common\n\n1675: common\n\n1676: leap\n\n1677: common\n\n1678: common\n\n1679: common\n\n1680: leap\n\n1681: common (1681 \u00f74=420.25, not leap)\n\nSo leap years are 1672, 1676, 1680. Again 3 leap years.\n\nTotal shift=11+3=14. 14 mod7=0. So Friday +0=Friday.\n\nWait, that seems strange. Wait, starting in 1671, the 11th anniversary is in 1682. Wait, hold on, no: the 11th anniversary would be in 1682, but the years we need to check are from 1671 to 1681 inclusive for the leap years. Wait, when starting in 1671, the 11th anniversary is 1682. Therefore, the shifts are determined by the years 1671 to 1681 inclusive. Wait, no: each shift is determined by the