{b} \\right) = 0\\), it implies:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{a^3}{b} - (x+y+z)a^2 + (xy + yz + zx)ab - xyz b^2 = 0\n   \\]\n\n4. **Integer Nature**:\n   Here, except \\(\\frac{a^3}{b}\\), all terms are integers. Therefore, \\(\\frac{a^3}{b}\\) must also be an integer, implying:\n   \\[\n   b = 1\n   \\]\n   Therefore:\n   \\[\n   x = a, \\quad a \\text{ is a positive integer}\n   \\]\n   Similarly, \\(y\\) and \\(z\\) must also be positive integers.\n\n5. **Exploring Values of \\(x, y, z\\)**:\n   Assume \\(x\\):\n\n   - **Case \\(x = 1\\)**:\n     \\[\n     1 + \\frac{1}{y} + \\frac{1}{z} \\text{ is an integer}\n     \\]\n     Hence, \\(\\frac{1}{y} + \\frac{1}{z}\\) is an integer. If \\(y \\geq 3\\), then \\(z \\geq y \\geq 3\\), resulting in:\n     \\[\n     0 < \\frac{1}{y} + \\frac{1}{z} \\leq \\frac{1}{3} + \\frac{1}{3} \\leq \\frac{2}{3}\n     \\]\n     This cannot be an integer, thus \\(y \\leq 2\\).\n\n     - For \\(x = 1, y = 1\\):\n       \\[\n       z = 1 \\quad (\\text{Only solution})\n       \\]\n     - For \\(x = 1, y = 2\\):\n       \\[\n       z = 2 \\quad (\\text{Only solution})\n       \\]\n     So solutions are:\n     \\(\n     (1, 1, 1) \\quad \\text{and} \\quad (1, 2, 2)\n     \\)\n\n   - **Case \\(x = 2\\)**:\n     \\[\n     y \\leq 4\n     \\]\n     If \\(y = 2\\):\n     - No integer \\(z \\geq 2\\) yields \\(\\frac{1}{x} + \\frac{1}{y} + \\frac{1}{z}\\).\n\n     If \\(y = 3\\):\n     - \\(z = 6\\) \\quad (Only solution)\n\n     If \\(y = 4\\):\n     - \\(z = 4\\) \\quad (Only solution)\n\n     So solutions for \\(x = 2\\) are:\n     \\[\n     (2, 3, 6) \\quad \\text{and} \\quad (2, 4, 4)\n     \\]\n\n   - **Case \\(x = 3\\)**:\n     \\[\n     y \\geq 3\n     \\]\n     If \\(y = 3\\):\n     - \\(z = 3\\) \\quad (Only solution)\n\n     For \\(y \\geq 4\\):\n     \\[\n     \\frac{1}{4} + \\frac{1}{4} + \\cdots < 1 \\]\n     yields no solutions.\n\nFinal collected positive rational triplets \\((x, y, z)\\) are:\n\\[\n(1, 1, 1), (1, 2, 2), (2, 3, 6), (2, 