,...,p_k. So for any n, P(n) is a product of primes from that finite set. But the polynomial P(n) has degree at least 1, so |P(n)| tends to infinity as n tends to infinity. But the number of integers composed of primes p_1,...,p_k is finite (as each such integer is of the form p_1^{e_1}...p_k^{e_k}), so |P(n)| would have to repeat values infinitely often, which is impossible for a non-constant polynomial. Therefore, this leads to a contradiction. Hence, there must be infinitely many primes dividing some P(n).\n\nWait, this seems like a possible approach. Let me formalize it:\n\nAssume that there are only finitely many primes p_1, p_2, ..., p_k that divide P(n) for some n. Then, for every positive integer n, P(n) is \u00b1 a product of these primes. Since the polynomial P(n) is non-constant, |P(n)| tends to infinity as n increases. However, the number of distinct values that P(n) can take, given that all prime factors are among p_1,...,p_k, is limited. Specifically, each P(n) can be written as \u00b1 p_1^{e_1}...p_k^{e_k}, where the exponents e_i are non-negative integers. The number of such integers with absolute value less than or equal to some N is roughly (log N)^k, which grows much slower than the linear (or higher) growth of |P(n)|. Therefore, beyond some N_0, |P(n)| exceeds all such products, leading to a contradiction. Hence, there must be infinitely many primes dividing some P(n).\n\nTherefore, the answer is yes: for every polynomial P of degree \u22651 with integer coefficients, there exist infinitely many primes that each divide at least one P(n) for some positive integer n.\n\nBut wait, let me check if this argument is valid. The key idea is that if only finitely many primes divided any P(n), then P(n) would have to be a product of those primes raised to some exponents. But the growth rate of P(n) is polynomial, which is faster than exponential in the exponents of a fixed set of primes. Therefore, beyond some point, P(n) would have to take a value not factorable into those primes, which would require a new prime, hence a contradiction. This is similar to Euclid's proof of the infinitude of primes.\n\nYes, this makes sense. So the conclusion is that there must be infinitely many primes dividing P(n) for some n. Therefore, the answer is yes.", "deepseek_solution": "For every polynomial \\( P \\) of degree \\( \\geq 1 \\) with integer coefficients, there exist infinitely many primes that divide at least one \\( P(n) \\) for some positive integer \\( n \\). This conclusion follows from the following reasoning:\n\n1. **Growth of Polynomial Values**: A non-constant polynomial \\( P(n) \\) grows without bound as \\( n \\to \\infty \\). Specifically, \\( |P(n)| \\) tends to infinity.\n\n2. **Assumption of Finitely Many Primes**: Suppose, for contradiction, that only finitely many primes \\( p_1, p_2, \\ldots, p_k \\) divide any \\( P(n) \\). Then, every value \\( P(n) \\) must be expressible as \\( \\pm p_1^{e_1}p_2^{e_2}\\cdots p_k^{e_k} \\), where \\( e_i \\geq 0 \\).\n\n3. **Limited Number of Factorizations**: The number of distinct integers formed by such products up to magnitude \\( N \\) grows polynomially (approximately \\( (\\log N)^k \\)), while \\( |P(n)| \\) grows polynomially (degree \\( \\geq 1 \\)). For large \\( N \\), the polynomial \\( |P(n)| \\) exceeds the number of possible factorizations, leading to a contradiction: \\( P(n) \\) would have to repeat values or include new primes, neither of which is possible.\n\n4. **Conclusion**: The assumption of finitely many primes is false. Hence, infinitely many primes must divide some \\( P(n) \\).\n\n**Final Answer**: \\(\\boxed{\\text{Yes}}\\)", "ground_truth_solution": "\nWe will prove that there exist infinitely many prime numbers that divide at least one value \\( P(n) \\) for a positive integer \\( n \\), for any polynomial \\( P \\) of degree \\(\\geq 1\\) with integer coefficients.\n\nLet \\( P(x) = a_m x^m + a_{m-1} x^{m-1} + \\ldots + a_1 x + a_0 \\) with \\( m \\geq 1 \\), \\( a_m \\neq 0 \\), and all \\( a_i \\in \\mathbb{Z} \\).\n\n1. **Case 1: \\( a_0 = 0 \\)**\n\n   - If \\( a_0 = 0 \\), then \\( P(x) \\) has \\( x \\) as a factor, i.e., \\( P(x) = xQ(x) \\) where \\( Q(x) \\) is a polynomial with integer coefficients. Therefore, for any prime \\( p \\), \\( p \\) divides \\( P(p) = p Q(p) \\).\n\n2. **Case 2: \\( a_0 \\neq 0 \\)**\n\n   In this case, \\( P(x) \\neq 0 \\) for all \\( x \\).\n\n   - Consider the polynomial \\( P(a_0^2 x) \\). We can write:\n     \\[\n     P(a_0^2 x) = a_m (a_0^2 x)^m + a_{m-1} (a_0^2 x)^{m-1} + \\ldots + a_1 (a_0^2 x) + a_0\n     \\]\n     which simplifies to:\n     \\[\n     P(a_0^2 x) = a_0 R(x)\n     \\]\n     where \\( R(x) = a_m a_0^{2m-1} x^m + a_{m-1} a_0^{2(m-1)} x^{m-1} + \\ldots + a_1 a_0^2 x + 1 \\).\n\n3. **Inductive Construction of Infinitely Many Primes:**\n\n   - **Base Case:**\n     - Since \\( |R(n)| \\to \\infty \\) as \\( n \\to \\infty \\), there exists a positive integer \\( n_1 \\) such that \\( R(n_1) \\) is non-zero and a prime \\( p_1 \\) divides \\( R(n_1) \\). Hence, \\( p_1 \\) also divides \\( P(a_0^2 n_1) \\).\n\n   - **Inductive Step:**\n\n     - **Inductive Hypothesis:**\n       Suppose we have already found primes \\( p_1, p_2, \\ldots, p_N \\) that each divide some \\( P(a_0^2 n_i) \\).\n\n     - **Inductive Proof:**\n       Consider \\( \\xi_k = |R(k \\cdot p_1 \\cdot p_2 \\cdots p_N)| \\) as \\( k \\to \\infty \\). Since \\( \\xi_k \\to \\infty \\), there exists a positive integer \\( k \\) such that \\( \\xi_k \\) is non-zero and a prime \\( p \\) divides \\( \\xi_k \\).\n       - This prime \\( p \\) does not divide any of \\( p_1, p_2, \\ldots, p_N \\), because \\( R(x) \\) evaluated at \\( k \\cdot p_1 \\cdot p_2 \\cdots p_N \\) would otherwise be divisible by those primes, but our polynomial construction ensures that this cannot happen indefinitely without encountering a new prime.\n\n     - Since \\( p \\) divides \\( \\xi_k = R(k \\cdo