82 > 3. Similarly, x\u2083 = \u221a(2x\u2082 + 3) > \u221a(6 + 3) = 3. So all terms are greater than 3.\n\nNow, check if the sequence is decreasing. Need to show x\u2096 < x\u2096\u208b\u2081 for all k \u2265 2.\n\nStart with x\u2082 and x\u2081: x\u2082 = \u221a(2x\u2081 + 3) < x\u2081 ?\n\nIf x\u2081 > 3, does \u221a(2x\u2081 + 3) < x\u2081 ?\n\nSquare both sides: 2x\u2081 + 3 < x\u2081\u00b2\n\nWhich rearranges to x\u2081\u00b2 - 2x\u2081 - 3 > 0\n\nFactor: (x\u2081 - 3)(x\u2081 + 1) > 0\n\nSince x\u2081 > 3, then x\u2081 - 3 > 0 and x\u2081 + 1 > 0, so the product is positive. Hence, the inequality holds.\n\nThus, x\u2082 < x\u2081.\n\nAssume x\u2096 < x\u2096\u208b\u2081 for some k \u2265 2, and x\u2096\u208b\u2081 > 3. Then x\u2096\u208a\u2081 = \u221a(2x\u2096 + 3). Since x\u2096 < x\u2096\u208b\u2081, then 2x\u2096 + 3 < 2x\u2096\u208b\u2081 + 3. Therefore, x\u2096\u208a\u2081 = \u221a(2x\u2096 + 3) < \u221a(2x\u2096\u208b\u2081 + 3) = x\u2096. Hence, by induction, the sequence is decreasing.\n\nFurthermore, since all terms are greater than 3, the sequence is bounded below by 3. Therefore, by the Monotone Convergence Theorem, it converges. The limit L must satisfy L = \u221a(2L + 3), so again L = 3.\n\nTherefore, regardless of the starting value x\u2081 (as long as x\u2081 is non-negative to keep the sequence real), the sequence converges to 3.\n\nBut wait, the problem didn't specify x\u2081. It just says \"the infinite sequence defined by x\u2081 and x\u2099 = ...\". So x\u2081 is an arbitrary initial term. But the square root requires that 2x\u2099\u208b\u2081 + 3 \u2265 0 for all n. Since x\u2099\u208b\u2081 is a square root, it's non-negative, so 2x\u2099\u208b\u2081 + 3 \u2265 3 > 0. Therefore, the sequence is well-defined for any x\u2081 \u2265 0, but actually, even if x\u2081 were negative, but then x\u2082 would require 2x\u2081 + 3 \u2265 0. So x\u2081 \u2265 -3/2. Wait, but the problem didn't specify x\u2081's domain. However, given that the sequen