gned}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Solve for \\( x \\):**\n\n   Combine the terms on the left side:\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   3x &= 180.\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n   Divide both sides by 3:\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   x &= 60.\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n   Hence, \\( x = 60 \\). This means \\( 2x = 120 \\).\n\n   Thus, there are 60 caps in the red box and 120 caps in the blue box.\n\n5. **Find the Combinations:**\n\n   We must determine which combination of the collected caps sums to 60 (for the red box) and 120 (for the blue box):\n\n   We test different combinations to find that:\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   29 + 31 &= 60,\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n   So, 29 and 31 caps should go into the red box.\n\n   The remaining caps are:\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   20 + 49 + 51 &= 120.\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n   Thus, 20, 49, and 51 caps go into the blue box.\n\n6. **Conclude:**\n\n   Therefore, the arrangement of caps in the boxes is:\n   - In the red box: Sv\u011btlana (29 caps) and Marta (31 caps).\n   - In the blue box: \u0160\u00e1rka (20 caps), Maru\u0161ka (49 caps), and Monika (51 caps).\n\n### Conclusion\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{In the red box: Sv\u011btlana and Marta; In the blue box: \u0160\u00e1rka, Maru\u0161ka, and Monika.}}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Points \\( A, B \\) and \\( C \\) lie on one line, and points \\( A_{1}, B_{1} \\) and \\( C_{1} \\) lie on another line. Prove that if \\( A B_{1} \\parallel B A_{1} \\) and \\( A C_{1} \\parallel C A_{1} \\), then \\( B C_{1} \\parallel C B_{1} \\).", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to prove that if points A, B, C are on one line, and points A\u2081, B\u2081, C\u2081 are on another line, and if AB\u2081 is parallel to BA\u2081 and AC\u2081 is parallel to CA\u2081, then BC\u2081 is parallel to CB\u2081. Hmm. Let me start by visualizing this. Maybe drawing a diagram would help. Let me imagine two lines, one with po