ck neighbors within A and one white neighbor in B. So total four neighbors, three black. Therefore, it satisfies the condition. Similarly, the interface cube in subcube B (black) has three white neighbors within B and one black neighbor in A. So total four neighbors, three white. Therefore, it also satisfies the condition.\n\nWait, this might work! If we tile the n x n x n cube (n even) with 2x2x2 subcubes, each colored in a checkerboard pattern, and arrange these subcubes in a larger checkerboard pattern, then each interface cube between subcubes has neighbors in both subcubes. The key is that the larger checkerboard ensures that adjacent subcubes are colored oppositely, so the interface cubes between subcubes are of opposite colors, maintaining the required neighbor count.\n\nLet's formalize this. For the entire cube, divide it into 2x2x2 subcubes. Assign to each subcube a color based on its position in the larger cube: if the subcube's coordinates (in terms of subcube indices) sum to even, color it with the standard checkerboard pattern starting with white at the subcube's (0,0,0). If the sum is odd, color it with the inverted checkerboard pattern (starting with black at the subcube's (0,0,0)).\n\nThis way, adjacent subcubes in the larger cube will have opposite checkerboard patterns, ensuring that interface cubes between subcubes are of opposite colors. Now, consider a cube at the interface between two subcubes. Within its own subcube, it's part of the checkerboard pattern and has three neighbors of the opposite color. The interface neighbor in the adjacent subcube is of the opposite color due to the larger checkerboard. However, the cube in question is on the face of its subcube, so within the subcube, it has three neighbors (corner or edge), and one neighbor in the adjacent subcube. Wait, no, in a 2x2x2 subcube, each cube has three neighbors. But when placed in the larger cube, each cube on the face of a subcube will have one neighbor in the adjacent subcube. 