that satisfy the conditions for \\(q < 0\\), hence the answer is no.\n\n### Final Answer\n\na) \\(\\boxed{\\text{Yes}}\\)\n\nb) \\(\\boxed{\\text{No}}\\)", "ground_truth_solution": "\nLet us consider two quadratic equations:\n\\[ x^2 - px + q = 0 \\]\n\\[ x^2 - (p+1)x + q = 0 \\]\n\nWe are to determine whether all roots of these equations can be integer values under two circumstances: \\( q > 0 \\) (part a) and \\( q < 0 \\) (part b).\n\n#### Part a: \\( q > 0 \\)\n\n1. Consider the polynomial \\( x^2 - 7x + 12 = 0 \\).\n    - The roots of this equation can be found using the quadratic formula, but also by noticing that \\( x = 3 \\) and \\( x = 4 \\) are integers:\n    \\[ (x-3)(x-4) = 0 \\Rightarrow x = 3, 4 \\]\n\n2. Consider the polynomial \\( x^2 - 8x + 12 = 0 \\).\n    - The roots of this equation can similarly be observed to be \\( x = 2 \\) and \\( x = 6 \\) (factors of 12 that add to 8):\n    \\[ (x-2)(x-6) = 0 \\Rightarrow x = 2, 6 \\]\n\n3. Both of these examples use specific values of \\( p \\) and positive \\( q \\) indicating that it is possible for both polynomials to have integer roots.\n\nHence, for \\( q > 0 \\):\n\\[\\boxed{\\text{Yes}} \\]\n\n#### Part b: \\( q < 0 \\)\n\n1. Assume \\( q < 0 \\). According to Vieta's formulas, for the equations \\( x^2 - px + q = 0 \\) and \\( x^2 - (p+1)x + q = 0 \\), the products of the roots are given by:\n    \\[ x_1 x_2 = q \\]\n    \\[ x_3 x_4 = q \\]\n\n2. Since \\( q < 0 \\), the roots must have opposite signs (one positive and one negative) for each equation.\n    - Let \\( x_1 > 0 \\) and \\( x_2 < 0 \\) be the roots of the first equation.\n    - Similarly, let \\( x_3 > 0 \\) and \\( x_4 < 0 \\) be the roots of the second equation.\n\n3. From Vieta's formulas, we have:\n    \\[ x_1 + x_2 = p \\]\n    \\[ x_3 + x_4 = p + 1 \\]\n\n4. Establish that \\( x_1 \\neq x_3 \\) (if they were equal, the equations would essentially be the same but translated).\n\n5. Order the roots such that \\( x_1 < x_3 \\). Therefore, we must have:\n    \\[ -x_2 > -x_4 \\Rightarrow x_2 < x_4 \\]\n   - Both \\( x_3 - x_1 \\geq 1 \\) and \\( x_4 - x_2 \\geq 1 \\).\n  \n6. By Vieta's again, subtract the sums:\n    \\[ (p+1) - p = (x_3 + x_4) - (x_1 + x_2) = (x_3 - x_1) + (x_4 - x_2) \\geq 2 \\]\n\n7. However, \\( (p+1) - p = 1 \\). This contradiction proves that our initial assumption must be incorrect.\n\nTherefore, for \\( q < 0 \\):\n\\[ \\boxed{\\text{No}} \\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Let \\( a_{1}, \\ldots, a_{n} \\) be real numbers. Show that \\( a_{1}^{2} + \\cdots + a_{n}^{2} \\geqslant \\frac{\\left(a_{1} + \\cdots + a_{n}\\right)^{2}}{n} \\).", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to show that the sum of the squares of some real numbers is greater than or equal to the square of their sum divided by n. Hmm, let me think. I remember there are some inequalities like Cauchy-Schwarz or maybe the QM-AM inequality. Let me recall. The QM-AM inequality says that the quadratic mean is greater than or equal to the arithmetic mean. Wait, quadratic mean is sqrt((a1\u00b2 + ... + an\u00b2)/n) and arithmetic mean is (a1 + ... + an)/n. So if I square both sides of QM \u2265 AM, that would give me (a1\u00b2 + ... + an\u00b2)/n \u2265 ((a1 + ... + an)/n)\u00b2. Then multiplying both sides by n would give exactly the inequality I need: a1\u00b2 + ... + an\u00b2 \u2265 (a1 + ... + an)\u00b2 / n. So maybe this is a direct application of QM-AM inequality. But maybe the problem expects me to prove it from scratch instead of just citing the inequality? Let me check.\n\nAlternatively, I can think of expanding the right-hand side and comparing term by term. Let's see. The left-hand side is sum_{i=1}^n ai\u00b2. The right-hand side is (sum_{i=1}^n ai)\u00b2 / n. Let me expand the square in the numerator: (sum ai)\u00b2 = sum ai\u00b2 + 2 sum_{i<j} ai aj. So then the right-hand side becomes [sum ai\u00b2 + 2 sum_{i<j} ai aj] / n. Therefore, the inequality becomes sum ai\u00b2 \u2265 [sum ai\u00b2 + 2 sum_{i<j} ai aj] / n. Let me multiply both sides by n to eliminate the denominator: n sum ai\u00b2 \u2265 sum ai\u00b2 + 2 sum_{i<j} ai aj. Subtracting sum ai\u00b2 from both sides gives (n - 1) sum ai\u00b2 \u2265 2 sum_{i<j} ai aj. Hmm, not sure if that's helpful. Maybe rearranging terms? Let me bring everything to one side: sum ai\u00b2 - [ (sum ai)^2 / n ] \u2265 0. Maybe that expression can be rewritten in a way that's obviously non-negative. Let's try.\n\nAnother approach: consider the variance. The variance of the numbers a1, ..., an is given by (1/n) sum (ai - mean)^2, where mean is (a1 + ... + an)/n. Expanding that, variance is (1/n) sum (ai\u00b2 - 2 ai mean + mean\u00b2) = (1/n) sum ai\u00b2 - 2 mean (1/n) sum ai + (1/n) sum mean\u00b2. Since sum ai = n mean, the second term becomes -2 mean\u00b2, and the third term is mean\u00b2. So variance = (1/n) sum ai\u00b2 - mean\u00b2. Since variance is always non-negative, this implies (1/n) sum ai\u00b2 \u2265 mean\u00b2. Multiplying both sides by n gives sum ai\u00b2 \u2265 n mean\u00b2 = (sum ai)^2 / n. So that's another way to see it using variance. But maybe the problem expects a more elementary proof.\n\nAlternatively, use Cauchy-Schwarz inequality. The Cauchy-Schwarz inequality states that (sum xi yi)^2 \u2264 (sum xi\u00b2)(sum yi\u00b2). If I set xi = ai and yi = 1 for all i, then (sum ai * 1)^2 \u2264 (sum ai\u00b2)(sum 1\u00b2). So (sum ai)^2 \u2264 (sum ai\u00b2)(n). Therefore, rearranged, sum ai\u00b2 \u2265 (sum ai)^2 / n. That works too. So that's another way to apply Cauchy-Schwarz.\n\nBut perhaps the problem wants an algebraic proof without invoking inequalities. Let's try expanding and rearranging. Let me denote S = a1 + a2 + ... + an. Then the inequality is sum ai\u00b2 \u2265 S\u00b2 / n. Let me consider sum (ai - S/n)^2. Expanding that gives sum [ai\u00b2 - 2 ai S/n + (S/n)^2] = sum ai\u00b2 - 2 S/n sum ai + n (S/n)^2. Since sum ai = S, this becomes sum ai\u00b2 - 2 S\u00b2 / n + S\u00b2 / n = sum ai\u00b2 - S\u00b2 / n. Since the left-hand side is the sum of squares of real numbers, it must be non-negative. Therefore, sum ai\u00b2 - S\u00b2 / n \u2265 0, which implies sum ai\u00b2 \u2265 S\u00b2 / n. That's a nice algebraic proof by considering the sum of squared deviations from the mean. So this shows the inequality holds because the sum of squares is minimized when all ai are equal, and otherwise, it's larger.\n\nSo in summary, there are multiple approaches: using QM-AM inequality, Cauchy-Schwarz, variance, or expanding the square of deviations. The problem is a classic example of an inequality that can be proven in various ways. Depending on the background knowledge, different methods might be preferred. For someone familiar with statistics, the variance approach is intuitive. For algebra, expanding the square works. For those who know inequalities, Cauchy-Schwarz or QM-AM are straightforward.\n\nI think the most straightforward and elementary proof is the one where you expand sum (ai - S/n)^2, show it's non-negative, and thus derive the inequality. It requires minimal prerequisites\u2014just expanding squares and knowing that squares are non-negative. Let me verify that again.\n\nLet S = a1 + ... + an. Then sum (ai - S/n)^2 = sum ai\u00b2 - 2(S/n) sum ai + sum (S\u00b2/n\u00b2). Since sum ai = S, the middle term is -2(S/n)*S = -2S\u00b2/n. The last term is n*(S\u00b2/n\u00b2) = S\u00b2/n. Therefore, sum (ai - S/n)^2 = sum ai\u00b2 - 2S\u00b2/n + S\u00b2/n = sum ai\u00b2 - S\u00b2/n. Since squares are non-negative, the left side is \u2265 0, so sum ai\u00b2 - S\u00b2/n \u2265 0 \u21d2 sum ai\u00b2 \u2265 S\u00b2/n. Yep, that works. This seems like a solid proof. The key idea is to relate the given inequality to the sum of squares of deviations from the mean, which is a fundamental concept in statistics. By recognizing that this sum must be non-negative, the inequality follows directly. \n\nAlternatively, using Cauchy-Schwarz: let vectors u = (a1, ..., an) and v = (1, 1, ..., 1). Then (u \u00b7 v)^2 \u2264 (u \u00b7 u)(v \u00b7 v). Here, u \u00b7 v = sum ai, u \u00b7 u = sum ai\u00b2, and v \u00b7 v = n. Therefore, (sum ai)^2 \u2264 sum ai\u00b2 * n, which rearranges to the desired inequality. That's also concise but requires knowledge of Cauchy-Schwarz. \n\nBut the algebraic proof is probably the most accessible since it doesn't require prior knowledge of inequalities. So depending on the audience, either method is suitable. Since the problem just says \"show that\", either approach is valid. However, the algebraic method is self-contained and perhaps more satisfying as a standalone proof.", "deepseek_solution": "To prove that \\( a_{1}^{2} + \\cdots + a_{n}^{2} \\geqslant \\frac{\\left(a_{1} + \\cdots + a_{n}\\right)^{2}}{n} \\) for real numbers \\( a_{1}, \\ldots, a_{n} \\), we use an algebraic approach involving the sum of squared deviations from the mean:\n\n1. **Define the sum \\( S \\):**  \n   Let \\( S = a_{1} + a_{2} + \\cdots + a_{n} \\).\n\n2. **Expand the sum of squared deviations:**  \n   Consider the expression \\( \\sum_{i=1}^{n} \\left( a_{i} - \\frac{S}{n} \\right)^2 \\). Expanding this gives:\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{i=1}^{n} \\left( a_{i}^2 - \\frac{2S}{n}a_{i} + \\frac{S^2}{n^2} \\right) = \\sum_{i=1}^{n} a_{i}^2 - \\frac{2S}{n} \\sum_{i=1}^{n} a_{i} + \\sum_{i=1}^{n} \\frac{S^2}{n^2}.\n   \\]\n\n3. **Simplify using \\( \\sum a_{i} = S \\):**  \n   Substitute \\( \\sum_{i=1}^{n} a_{i} = S \\):\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{i=1}^{n} a_{i}^2 - \\frac{2S}{n} \\cdot S + n \\cdot \\frac{S^2}{n^2} = \\sum_{i=1}^{n} a_{i}^2 - \\frac{2S^2}{n} + \\frac{S^2}{n}.\n   \\]\n\n4. **Combine terms:**  \n   This simplifies to:\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{i=1}^{n} a_{i}^2 - \\frac{S^2}{n}.\n   \\]\n\n5. **Apply non-negativity of squares:**  \n   Since squares are non-negative:\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{i=1}^{n} \\left( a_{i} - \\frac{S}{n} \\right)^2 \\geq 0 \\implies \\sum_{i=1}^{n} a_{i}^2 - \\frac{S^2}{n} \\geq 0.\n   \\]\n\n6. **Final inequality:**  \n   Rearranging gives:\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{i=1}^{n} a_{i}^2 \\geq \\frac{S^2}{n} = \\frac{\\left( a_{1} + \\cdots + a_{n} \\right)^2}{n}.\n   \\]\n\n**Conclusion:**  \nThe inequality \\( a_{1}^{2} + \\cdots + a_{n}^{2} \\geqslant \\frac{\\left(a_{1} + \\cdots + a_{n}\\right)^{2}}{n} \\) holds because the sum of squared deviations from the mean is non-negative. \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{a_{1}^{2} + \\cdots + a_{n}^{2} \\geqslant \\frac{\\left(a_{1} + \\cdots + a_{n}\\right)^{2}}{n}}\n\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "\nTo show that \n\n\\[ a_{1}^{2} + a_{2}^{2} + \\cdots + a_{n}^{2} \\geqslant \\frac{\\left( a_{1} + a_{2} + \\cdots + a_{n} \\right)^{2}}{n}, \\]\n\nwe will apply the **Cauchy-Schwarz inequality**.\n\n1. **Set-up for Cauchy-Schwarz:**\n\n   Consider two sequences \\( e_i = a_i \\) and \\( f_i = 1 \\) for \\( i = 1, 2, \\ldots, n \\). According to the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality in \\(\\mathbb{R}^n\\):\n\n   \\[ \n   \\left( \\sum_{i=1}^{n} e_i f_i \\right)^2 \\leq \\left( \\sum_{i=1}^{n} e_i^2 \\right) \\left( \\sum_{i=1}^{n} f_i^2 \\right) \n   \\]\n\n2. **Apply with our sequences:**\n\n   Substituting \\( e_i = a_i \\) and \\( f_i = 1 \\),\n   \n   \\[\n   \\left( \\sum_{i=1}^{n} a_i \\cdot 1 \\right)^2 \\leq \\left( \\sum_{i=1}^{n} a_i^2 \\right) \\left( \\sum_{i=1}^{n} 1^2 \\right)\n   \\]\n\n3. **Simplification:**\n\n   \\[\n   \\left( \\sum_{i=1}^{n} a_i \\right)^2 \\leq \\left( \\sum_{i=1}^{n} a_i^2 \\right) \\left( \\sum_{i=1}^{n} 1 \\right)\n   \\]\n   \n   Clearly, \\( \\sum_{i=1}^{n} 1 = n \\). Thus,\n\n   \\[\n   \\left( \\sum_{i=1}^{n} a_i \\right)^2 \\leq \\left( \\sum_{i=1}^{n} a_i^2 \\right) n\n   \\]\n\n4. **Rearrange for the desired inequality:**\n\n   Dividing both sides by \\( n \\),\n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{\\left( \\sum_{i=1}^{n} a_i \\right)^2}{n} \\leq \\sum_{i=1}^{n} a_i^2\n   \\]\n\n   which can be written as:\n\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{i=1}^{n} a_i^2 \\geq \\frac{\\left( \\sum_{i=1}^{n} a_i \\right)^2}{n}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Equality Condition:**\n\n   We have equality in the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality if and only if there exists a real number \\( \\lambda \\) such that for all \\( i \\), \n\n   \\[ \n   a_i = \\lambda \\cdot 1 \\Rightarrow a_i = \\lambda\n   \\]\n\n   Hence, in our case, equality holds if and only if all \\( a_i \\)'s are equal.\n\n### Conclusion:\n\n\\[ \na_{1}^{2} + a_{2}^{2} + \\cdots + a_{n}^{2} \\geqslant \\frac{\\left( a_{1} + a_{2} + \\cdots + a_{n} \\right)^{2}}{n}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{a_{1}^{2} + a_{2}^{2} + a_{3}^{2} + \\cdots + a_{n}^{2} \\geq \\frac{\\left( a_{1} + a_{2} + \\cdots + a_{n} \\right)^2}{n}}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "In triangle \\(ABC\\), points \\(P\\) and \\(Q\\) are taken on the base \\(AC\\) such that \\(AP < AQ\\). The lines \\(BP\\) and \\(BQ\\) divide the median \\(AM\\) into three equal parts. It is known that \\(PQ = 3\\).\n\nFind \\(AC\\).", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, let me try to figure out how to solve this geometry problem. So we have triangle ABC, with points P and Q on the base AC such that AP is less than AQ. The lines BP and BQ divide the median AM into three equal parts. We know that PQ is 3, and we need to find the length of AC.\n\nFirst, let me visualize the problem. Triangle ABC, with base AC. AM is the median from A to BC, so M is the midpoint of BC. Points P and Q are on AC, with AP < AQ, meaning P is closer to A than Q is. The lines BP and BQ intersect the median AM and divide it into three equal parts. So if AM is divided into three equal parts, the points where BP and BQ intersect AM would be at 1/3 and 2/3 of AM's length from A, right?\n\nWait, but the problem says BP and BQ divide AM into three equal parts. So does that mean that the two intersection points split AM into three segments of equal length? So if we let the intersection points be, say, D and E, then AD = DE = EM? So AM is divided into three parts each of length AM/3.\n\nYes, that makes sense. So BP and BQ intersect AM at D and E, which are spaced such that AD = DE = EM. Therefore, AD is 1/3 of AM, DE is another 1/3, and EM is the last 1/3.\n\nGiven that, we need to relate the positions of P and Q on AC to these division points on AM. Since AP < AQ, then P is closer to A than Q. So BP is the line from B to P, which is closer to A, and BQ is the line from B to Q, which is further along AC. These two lines intersect the median AM at points that trisect it. Therefore, maybe BP intersects AM at D (1/3 from A) and BQ intersects AM at E (2/3 from A). Or is it the other way around? Let me think.\n\nIf AP < AQ, then point P is closer to A, so BP would be a line from B to a point near A. Similarly, BQ is a line from B to a point Q further along AC. So when these lines intersect AM, which is the median from A to M (midpoint of BC), perhaps BP intersects AM closer to A, and BQ intersects AM further from A. Therefore, BP would intersect AM at D (1/3 from A), and BQ would intersect AM at E (2/3 from A). That seems plausible.\n\nTherefore, the problem reduces to finding the positions of P and Q on AC such that BP and BQ intersect AM at 1/3 and 2/3 points. Then, given that PQ is 3, find AC.\n\nTo approach this, maybe coordinate geometry would help. Let me set up a coordinate system. Let me place point A at (0, 0), and point C at (c, 0) so that AC is along the x-axis. Then, since AM is the median, M is the midpoint of BC. But we don't know the coordinates of B yet. Let me denote point B as (d, e). Then M, being the midpoint of BC, would be at ((c + d)/2, e/2).\n\nBut since AM is a median, and we need to find its division points. Let's first find the coordinates of points D and E on AM that trisect it. Since A is (0, 0) and M is ((c + d)/2, e/2), the parametric equations of AM can be given by t*( (c + d)/2, e/2 ) where t ranges from 0 to 1. So the point D, which is 1/3 along AM, would be at ( (c + d)/6, e/6 ), and E, which is 2/3 along AM, would be at ( (c + d)/3, e/3 ).\n\nNow, we need to find points P and Q on AC such that BP passes through D and BQ passes through E.\n\nSince AC is from (0, 0) to (c, 0), any point on AC can be represented as (p, 0) where p is between 0 and c. Since AP < AQ, then point P is at (p, 0) and Q is at (q, 0) where 0 < p < q < c.\n\nSo BP is the line from B(d, e) to P(p, 0), and this line must pass through D( (c + d)/6, e/6 ). Similarly, BQ is the line from B(d, e) to Q(q, 0) passing through E( (c + d)/3, e/3 ).\n\nLet me write the equations of lines BP and BQ and use the fact that they pass through D and E respectively.\n\nFirst, let's handle BP. The line BP goes from (d, e) to (p, 0). Let's parametrize this line. A parametric equation can be written as:\n\nx = d + t(p - d)\ny = e + t(0 - e) = e(1 - t)\n\nwhere t ranges from 0 to 1.\n\nWe know this line passes through D( (c + d)/6, e/6 ). So there exists some parameter t such that:\n\nx = d + t(p - d) = (c + d)/6\n\ny = e(1 - t) = e/6\n\nFrom the y-coordinate equation: e(1 - t) = e/6. Assuming e \u2260 0 (otherwise, the triangle would be degenerate), we can divide both sides by e:\n\n1 - t = 1/6 \u21d2 t = 5/6\n\nNow substitute t = 5/6 into the x-coordinate equation:\n\nd + (5/6)(p - d) = (c + d)/6\n\nMultiply both sides by 6 to eliminate denominators:\n\n6d + 5(p - d) = c + d\n\nExpand:\n\n6d + 5p - 5d = c + d\n\nSimplify:\n\nd + 5p = c + d\n\nSubtract d from both sides:\n\n5p = c \u21d2 p = c/5\n\nInteresting, so point P is at (c/5, 0)\n\nNow let's handle BQ. Similarly, the line BQ goes from (d, e) to (q, 0). Let's parametrize this line:\n\nx = d + s(q - d)\ny = e + s(0 - e) = e(1 - s)\n\nThis line must pass through E( (c + d)/3, e/3 )\n\nSo setting the coordinates equal:\n\nx = d + s(q - d) = (c + d)/3\n\ny = e(1 - s) = e/3\n\nAgain, assuming e \u2260 0, from the y-coordinate equation:\n\n1 - s = 1/3 \u21d2 s = 2/3\n\nSubstitute s = 2/3 into the x-coordinate equation:\n\nd + (2/3)(q - d) = (c + d)/3\n\nMultiply both sides by 3:\n\n3d + 2(q - d) = c + d\n\nExpand:\n\n3d + 2q - 2d = c + d\n\nSimplify:\n\nd + 2q = c + d\n\nSubtract d from both sides:\n\n2q = c \u21d2 q = c/2\n\nSo point Q is at (c/2, 0)\n\nTherefore, points P and Q are located at c/5 and c/2 along AC. Therefore, the distance between P and Q is PQ = q - p = c/2 - c/5 = (5c - 2c)/10 = 3c/10\n\nBut we are given that PQ = 3, so 3c/10 = 3 \u21d2 c = 10\n\nTherefore, the length of AC is 10.\n\nWait, that seems straightforward. Let me double-check.\n\nWe set up coordinates with A at (0,0), C at (c,0), B at (d,e). Found that when lines BP and BQ pass through the trisection points D and E of AM, the parameters t and s give positions of P and Q as c/5 and c/2. Then PQ is 3c/10 = 3 \u21d2 c=10. Seems correct.\n\nBut let me verify if the coordinates of D and E were correctly calculated.\n\nAM is from A(0,0) to M((c + d)/2, e/2). The trisection points would be at 1/3 and 2/3 of the way from A to M.\n\nSo coordinates:\n\nD: ( ( (c + d)/2 )*(1/3), (e/2)*(1/3) ) = ( (c + d)/6, e/6 )\n\nE: ( ( (c + d)/2 )*(2/3), (e/2)*(2/3) ) = ( (c + d)/3, e/3 )\n\nYes, that's correct.\n\nThen, for line BP passing through D, we found t = 5/6 leading to p = c/5. Similarly, for BQ passing through E, s = 2/3 leading to q = c/2. Then PQ = q - p = c/2 - c/5 = 3c/10 = 3 \u21d2 c=10. Therefore, AC = c = 10.\n\nThis seems correct, but maybe I should check with a different approach to confirm.\n\nAlternatively, using mass point geometry or similar triangles.\n\nLet me think. Since AM is divided into three equal parts by BP and BQ, maybe we can use coordinate geometry as before, but perhaps with specific coordinates to simplify calculations.\n\nAlternatively, set coordinates with A at (0,0), C at (c,0), and let\u2019s choose B at (0, b) to make it a right triangle. Wait, but then M would be at (c/2, 0), but no. If B is at (0, b), then BC is from (0, b) to (c, 0), so midpoint M is at (c/2, b/2). Then AM is the median from (0,0) to (c/2, b/2). Then the trisection points D and E would be at (c/6, b/6) and (c/3, b/3).\n\nThen, points P and Q are on AC, which is from (0,0) to (c,0). So P is (p, 0) and Q is (q, 0). Lines BP and BQ go from (0, b) to (p, 0) and (q, 0) respectively, passing through D(c/6, b/6) and E(c/3, b/3).\n\nLet me compute the equations of BP and BQ in this coordinate system.\n\nFirst, line BP: from (0, b) to (p, 0). The parametric equations:\n\nx = pt, y = b(1 - t), where t \u2208 [0,1]\n\nThis line must pass through D(c/6, b/6). So set x = pt = c/6 and y = b(1 - t) = b/6.\n\nFrom the y-coordinate: b(1 - t) = b/6 \u21d2 1 - t = 1/6 \u21d2 t = 5/6\n\nThen x = p*(5/6) = c/6 \u21d2 p = (c/6)*(6/5) = c/5\n\nSimilarly, line BQ from (0, b) to (q, 0). Parametric equations:\n\nx = qs, y = b(1 - s), s \u2208 [0,1]\n\nThis passes through E(c/3, b/3). So set x = qs = c/3 and y = b(1 - s) = b/3\n\nFrom y-coordinate: b(1 - s) = b/3 \u21d2 1 - s = 1/3 \u21d2 s = 2/3\n\nThen x = q*(2/3) = c/3 \u21d2 q = (c/3)*(3/2) = c/2\n\nTherefore, PQ = q - p = c/2 - c/5 = 3c/10. Given PQ = 3, so 3c/10 = 3 \u21d2 c = 10\n\nSame result. So regardless of the coordinate system, the result is consistent. Therefore, AC = 10.\n\nTherefore, the answer is 10. I think that's solid.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe length of \\(AC\\) is \\(\\boxed{10}\\).", "deepseek_solution": "Given triangle \\(ABC\\) with points \\(P\\) and \\(Q\\) on the base \\(AC\\) such that \\(AP < AQ\\). The lines \\(BP\\) and \\(BQ\\) divide the median \\(AM\\) into three equal parts. It is known that \\(PQ = 3\\). We need to find the length of \\(AC\\).\n\n1. **Coordinate Setup**:\n   - Place \\(A\\) at \\((0, 0)\\), \\(C\\) at \\((c, 0)\\), and \\(B\\) at \\((d, e)\\).\n   - The midpoint \\(M\\) of \\(BC\\) is \\(\\left(\\frac{c + d}{2}, \\frac{e}{2}\\right)\\).\n\n2. **Trisection Points on \\(AM\\)**:\n   - The trisection points \\(D\\) and \\(E\\) on \\(AM\\) are at \\(\\left(\\frac{c + d}{6}, \\frac{e}{6}\\right)\\) and \\(\\left(\\frac{c + d}{3}, \\frac{e}{3}\\right)\\).\n\n3. **Equations of Lines \\(BP\\) and \\(BQ\\)**:\n   - Line \\(BP\\) from \\(B(d, e)\\) to \\(P(p, 0)\\) passing through \\(D\\left(\\frac{c + d}{6}, \\frac{e}{6}\\right)\\):\n     - Solving for parameter \\(t\\), we find \\(p = \\frac{c}{5}\\).\n   - Line \\(BQ\\) from \\(B(d, e)\\) to \\(Q(q, 0)\\) passing through \\(E\\left(\\frac{c + d}{3}, \\frac{e}{3}\\right)\\):\n     - Solving for parameter \\(s\\), we find \\(q = \\frac{c}{2}\\).\n\n4. **Distance \\(PQ\\)**:\n   - The distance \\(PQ = q - p = \\frac{c}{2} - \\frac{c}{5} = \\frac{3c}{10}\\).\n   - Given \\(PQ = 3\\), we solve \\(\\frac{3c}{10} = 3\\) to find \\(c = 10\\).\n\nThus, the length of \\(AC\\) is \\(\\boxed{10}\\).", "ground_truth_solution": "Consider the triangle \\(ABC\\) with points \\(P\\) and \\(Q\\) on the base \\(AC\\) such that \\(AP < AQ\\). The lines \\(BP\\) and \\(BQ\\) divide the median \\(AM\\) into three equal parts. We are given \\(PQ = 3\\).\n\n1. Draw a line through vertex \\(B\\) parallel to \\(AC\\) and extend the median \\(AM\\) to meet this line at point \\(T\\). Due to the symmetry and parallelism, we know that:\n   \\[\n   BT = AC \\text{ and } MT = AM\n   \\]\n\n2. Let \\(F\\) and \\(H\\) be the points of intersection of the median \\(AM\\) with segments \\(BP\\) and \\(BQ\\) respectively. Thus, we have:\n   \\[\n   AF = \\frac{1}{6} AT \\quad \\text{and} \\quad AH = \\frac{1}{3} AT\n   \\]\n\n3. By similarity of triangles \\(AFP\\) and \\(TFB\\), we establish the following ratio:\n   \\[\n   AP = \\frac{1}{5} BT = \\frac{1}{5} AC\n   \\]\n\n4. Similarly, using the similarity of triangles \\(AHQ\\) and \\(THB\\), we find:\n   \\[\n   AQ = \\frac{1}{2} BT = \\frac{1}{2} AC\n   \\]\n\n5. Since \\(AQ - AP = PQ = 3\\), plug in the expressions found:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{1}{2} AC - \\frac{1}{5} AC = 3\n   \\]\n\n6. Solve for \\(AC\\):\n   \\[\n   \\left( \\frac{1}{2} - \\frac{1}{5} \\right) AC = 3\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\frac{5}{10} - \\frac{2}{10} = \\frac{3}{10}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\frac{3}{10} AC = 3\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   AC = 3 \\cdot \\frac{10}{3} = 10\n   \\]\n\nSo, the length of \\(AC\\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{10}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "The sum of the lengths of several vectors in the plane is equal to \\(L\\). Prove that it is possible to choose some number of these vectors (possibly just one) such that the length of their sum is at least \\(L / \\pi\\).", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, let's see. The problem states that we have several vectors in the plane, and the sum of their lengths is L. We need to prove that there exists a subset of these vectors (maybe just one) such that the length of their sum is at least L/\u03c0. Hmm, interesting. \n\nFirst, let me make sure I understand the problem correctly. We have a bunch of vectors, each with some length, and if you add up all their lengths (not vectorially, just the magnitudes), that total is L. Now, we need to show that among all possible subsets of these vectors, there's at least one subset where when you add them up vectorially (i.e., considering their directions), the resulting vector has a length of at least L/\u03c0. \n\nSo, the key here is that even though the vectors might be pointing in different directions, there must be some combination where their vector sum is not too small. The challenge is to find such a subset. The bound given is L/\u03c0, which makes me think that maybe there's some connection to angles or maybe something related to integrating over a circle. Hmm, \u03c0 is involved, so maybe a geometric argument with angles?\n\nLet me think. Maybe if I consider the directions of the vectors. If all vectors are pointing in random directions, perhaps there's a way to select a subset whose directions are somewhat aligned, so their sum would be significant. But how do I formalize that?\n\nAlternatively, maybe using the pigeonhole principle. If we divide the circle into regions, maybe some region contains enough vectors that their sum in that direction is large. But how to set up the regions?\n\nAnother thought: if I take each vector and project it onto some direction, say the x-axis. Then the sum of the projections (with sign) would be the x-component of the total vector sum. But the problem is about the maximum possible projection over all possible subsets. Wait, but we need the maximum length of the vector sum, which is equivalent to the maximum projection over all possible directions. \n\nWait, maybe there's a connection here with the concept of the maximum projection. For any vector sum, its length is equal to the maximum projection onto any direction. So, if I can show that there exists a direction where the sum of some subset's projections is at least L/\u03c0, then the length of their sum in that direction would be at least L/\u03c0. \n\nBut how to choose the direction and the subset? Maybe if we consider all possible directions and average the maximum projection over those directions. Then, if the average is at least L/\u03c0, there must exist some direction where the maximum projection is at least L/\u03c0. \n\nAlternatively, maybe using probabilistic methods. Suppose we randomly select a subset of vectors, what's the expected length of their sum? But I don't know if that would directly give a lower bound. \n\nWait, let's think about another approach. Suppose we consider each vector as a complex number. Then, the problem reduces to selecting a subset of complex numbers such that the modulus of their sum is at least L/\u03c0. The total sum of moduli is L. \n\nIs there a theorem related to this? Maybe something in harmonic analysis or geometry. I recall there's a result called the \"vector sum problem\" or something similar, where you have vectors with certain properties and you can find a subset with a large sum. \n\nAlternatively, think about arranging the vectors in the plane and rotating the coordinate system such that their angles are considered relative to some axis. If we can cover the circle with angles and find that in some direction, the sum of the components of a subset is large.\n\nWait, here's an idea. Suppose we consider the unit circle and for each vector, we can represent its direction as an angle \u03b8. If we imagine integrating over all possible angles \u03b8 from 0 to 2\u03c0, the projection of each vector onto the direction \u03b8 is |v_i| cos(\u03b8 - \u03c6_i), where \u03c6_i is the angle of vector v_i. \n\nBut we need the maximum over \u03b8 of the maximum subset sum of |v_i| cos(\u03b8 - \u03c6_i). Maybe if we average over all \u03b8, and then use the fact that the average maximum subset projection is at least L/(2\u03c0), but we need L/\u03c0. Hmm, not sure.\n\nAlternatively, consider that for each vector v_i, when you project it onto a random direction \u03b8, the expected value of the projection is (2/\u03c0)|v_i|. Wait, is that true? Let me recall. The average value of |cos(\u03b8)| over \u03b8 from 0 to 2\u03c0 is 2/\u03c0. So, the expected value of the absolute projection is (2/\u03c0)|v_i|. So, if we pick a random direction \u03b8, then the expected value of the sum of absolute projections of all vectors is (2/\u03c0)L. \n\nBut we need a subset whose projection in some direction is large. Maybe we can use this expectation to argue that there exists a direction where the maximum subset sum is at least the expectation. Wait, but expectation is over random directions. \n\nAlternatively, for each direction \u03b8, define S(\u03b8) as the sum of all vectors projected onto \u03b8, considering their sign. Then, the maximum S(\u03b8) over \u03b8 would be the maximum projection in any direction. But we need the maximum over subsets. \n\nWait, perhaps if we consider for each direction \u03b8, the maximum subset sum is the sum of all vectors with positive projection in that direction. Then, if we can show that the average over all \u03b8 of these maximum subset sums is at least L/\u03c0, then there must exist a \u03b8 where the maximum subset sum is at least L/\u03c0. \n\nLet me formalize this. For each angle \u03b8, let's define the subset S(\u03b8) as the set of vectors whose projection onto \u03b8 is positive. Then, the sum of their projections is equal to the sum over all vectors of max(0, |v_i| cos(\u03b8 - \u03c6_i)). The integral over \u03b8 from 0 to 2\u03c0 of this sum would be the integral over \u03b8 of sum_{i} |v_i| max(0, cos(\u03b8 - \u03c6_i)) d\u03b8. \n\nSwitching the sum and the integral, this becomes sum_{i} |v_i| \u222b_{0}^{2\u03c0} max(0, cos(\u03b8 - \u03c6_i)) d\u03b8. But the integral of max(0, cos(\u03b8)) over 0 to 2\u03c0 is 2 (since cos(\u03b8) is positive in [-\u03c0/2, \u03c0/2], and the integral over that interval is 2). So, each term becomes |v_i| * 2. Therefore, the total integral is 2L. \n\nTherefore, the average over \u03b8 of the maximum subset projection (i.e., the integral divided by 2\u03c0) is (2L)/(2\u03c0) = L/\u03c0. \n\nHence, there must exist some \u03b8 where the maximum subset projection is at least L/\u03c0. Therefore, the length of the sum of the subset S(\u03b8) is at least L/\u03c0. \n\nWait, that seems to work! Let me recap to make sure. \n\nBy considering all possible directions \u03b8, and for each direction, taking the subset of vectors that have positive projection onto \u03b8, the average value (over \u03b8) of the sum of these projections is L/\u03c0. Therefore, by the average argument, there exists at least one \u03b8 where the sum of projections is at least L/\u03c0. Since the length of the vector sum is at least the absolute value of its projection onto any direction, in particular \u03b8, the length of the sum of the subset S(\u03b8) must be at least L/\u03c0. \n\nTherefore, such a subset exists. \n\nBut wait, let me check the step where I switched the sum and the integral. That's valid by linearity of integration, right? The integral of the sum is the sum of the integrals. So, that step is okay. \n\nAlso, the integral of max(0, cos(\u03b8)) over 0 to 2\u03c0: cos(\u03b8) is positive from -\u03c0/2 to \u03c0/2, so over a full circle, that's \u03c0 interval. But integrating cos(\u03b8) over -\u03c0/2 to \u03c0/2 gives 2. So, over 0 to 2\u03c0, it's the same as integrating over -\u03c0/2 to \u03c0/2, shifted by any angle, so each integral of max(0, cos(\u03b8 - \u03c6_i)) over \u03b8 from 0 to 2\u03c0 is 2. Therefore, that term is correct. \n\nSo, integrating over all \u03b8, we get 2L, and dividing by 2\u03c0 gives L/\u03c0. Therefore, there must exist some \u03b8 where the sum is at least L/\u03c0. \n\nTherefore, the subset of vectors with positive projection onto that \u03b8 direction has a vector sum of length at least L/\u03c0. \n\nTherefore, the proof is complete. \n\nI think that works. Let me see if there's another way to think about it, maybe using geometry or complex numbers. \n\nAlternatively, suppose we model each vector as a complex number z_i with |z_i| = l_i, and the total sum of l_i is L. We need to show that there exists a subset S such that |sum_{i in S} z_i| \u2265 L/\u03c0. \n\nUsing the probabilistic method, suppose we choose each vector independently with probability p. Then, the expected value of the sum is p * sum z_i, but the magnitude of the expectation is p * |sum z_i|. But this might not help. Alternatively, if we consider choosing a random subset, but I don't see a direct way. \n\nAlternatively, use the idea of duality. Since the problem is about existence of a subset with a certain property, maybe use linear algebra or convex geometry. But the previous averaging argument seems more straightforward. \n\nAnother approach: consider the vector sum as a point in the plane, and the set of all possible subset sums forms a symmetric set around the origin. We need to show that this set contains a point at distance at least L/\u03c0 from the origin. \n\nBut how to show that? Maybe by considering the area covered by these subset sums, but that might be complicated. \n\nAlternatively, use the pigeonhole principle. If all subset sums are within a circle of radius L/\u03c0, then the number of possible subset sums is limited, but since there are 2^n subsets, that might not help. \n\nWait, but the averaging argument seems the most promising and actually gives the result directly. So, in conclusion, by integrating the maximum projection over all directions and using the average value, we can guarantee the existence of such a subset. \n\nTherefore, the proof is valid.", "deepseek_solution": "To prove that there exists a subset of vectors whose sum has length at least \\( L/\\pi \\), we use an averaging argument over all possible directions in the plane:\n\n1. **Projection Analysis**: For any direction \\(\\theta\\), consider the subset of vectors with positive projections onto \\(\\theta\\). Let \\(S(\\theta)\\) denote their vector sum. The length of \\(S(\\theta)\\) is at least the absolute value of its projection onto \\(\\theta\\).\n\n2. **Integrate Over All Directions**: The average value of the maximum projection (over all \\(\\theta\\)) is computed by integrating the projections across all angles:\n   \\[\n   \\int_{0}^{2\\pi} \\text{max-projection}(\\theta) \\, d\\theta = \\sum_{i} |v_i| \\int_{0}^{2\\pi} \\max(0, \\cos(\\theta - \\phi_i)) \\, d\\theta,\n   \\]\n   where \\(\\phi_i\\) is the angle of vector \\(v_i\\). The integral of \\(\\max(0, \\cos\\theta)\\) over \\([0, 2\\pi]\\) is \\(2\\), so the total becomes \\(2L\\).\n\n3. **Average Projection**: Dividing by \\(2\\pi\\) gives the average maximum projection:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{2L}{2\\pi} = \\frac{L}{\\pi}.\n   \\]\n   By the averaging principle, there must exist some \\(\\theta\\) where the maximum projection (and thus the length of \\(S(\\theta)\\)) is at least \\(L/\\pi\\).\n\n**Conclusion**: Such a subset exists, ensuring the required bound. \n\n**Final Answer**\n\\boxed{\\dfrac{L}{\\pi}}", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. **Introduction**: Given a set of vectors in the plane with a total length \\( L \\), we aim to prove that we can select a subset of these vectors such that the length of their sum is at least \\( L / \\pi \\).\n\n2. **Key Insight**: To begin, we leverage a known result, which states that the average value of the sum of the lengths of the projections of these vectors onto any line is \\( \\frac{2L}{\\pi} \\).\n\n3. **Average Projection Calculation**:\n   Consider any line in the plane. The projection of a vector \\(\\mathbf{v}\\) onto this line creates a shadow along the line. The length of this projection depends on the angle \\(\\theta\\) between the vector and the line, calculated as \\( \\|\\mathbf{v}\\| \\cos(\\theta) \\). When summed over all vectors, the total length of these projections fluctuates with direction, but on average, it's equal to \\( \\frac{2L}{\\pi} \\).\n\n4. **Function Application**:\n   Let us formalize this with the function \\( f \\) representing the sum of projections along various directions:\n   \\[\n   f(\\theta) = \\sum_{i} \\|\\mathbf{v}_i\\| \\cos(\\theta_i - \\theta)\n   \\]\n   By integrating over all directions \\(\\theta\\), we link the total magnitude and average projection:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{1}{2\\pi} \\int_0^{2\\pi} f(\\theta) \\, d\\theta = \\frac{2L}{\\pi}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Intermediate Value Theorem**: To establish that this average projection is never uniformly less than its expected value throughout the interval, use the intermediate value theorem. Suppose \\( c \\) is the average value of \\( f(\\theta) \\) over the interval \\([0, 2\\pi]\\):\n   \\[\n   c = \\frac{1}{2\\pi} \\int_0^{2\\pi} f(\\theta) \\, d\\theta\n   \\]\n   If \\( f(\\theta) \\) were always less than \\( c \\), this would contradict the calculation of \\( c \\). Hence, there exists some direction \\( \\theta = l \\) where the sum of the projections is at least \\( \\frac{2L}{\\pi} \\).\n\n6. **Positive and Negative Projections**:\n   By examining directions, we can determine that if in one direction the positive projections provide a sum, in another, the negative projections would balance this:\n   \\[\n   \\sum_i \\|\\mathbf{v}_i\\| \\cos(\\theta_i) \\geq \\frac{L}{\\pi}\n   \\]\n   \n7. **Choose the Bounded Set**: \n   Given this, choosing the appropriate subset of vectors yielding positive projections or negative projections suffices to find subsets with sums whose lengths are at least \\( \\frac{L}{\\pi} \\).\n\n8. **Conclusion**:\n   This reasoning confirms that from any set of vectors with total length \\( L \\), there exists a subset of vectors with a combined length of their sum being no less than \\( \\frac{L}{\\pi} \\).\n\n\\(\\boxed{\\text{Hence, the claim is proven.}}\\)", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "How many times does the digit 9 appear in the answer to \\( 987654321 \\times 9 \\)?\nA 0\nB 1\nC 5\nD 8\nE 9", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, let's try to figure out how many times the digit 9 appears in the answer when we multiply 987654321 by 9. Hmm, the question gives options from 0 to 9, so the answer must be one of those. Let me start by actually performing the multiplication step by step.\n\nFirst, I know that multiplying a number by 9 is the same as multiplying by 10 and then subtracting the original number. So maybe that could help. Let me try that approach.\n\nThe original number is 987,654,321. If I multiply this by 10, it becomes 9,876,543,210. Then, subtracting the original number (987,654,321) from this result should give me the product of 987,654,321 \u00d7 9. Let me write that out:\n\n9,876,543,210\n-   987,654,321\n-----------------\n?\n\nLet me perform the subtraction. Starting from the rightmost digit:\n\n0 - 1: Hmm, can't do that, so I need to borrow. But this might get complicated with all the zeros. Wait, maybe there's a pattern here. Let me check if there's a different way.\n\nAlte