e also have\n\\[\nCB^2 = CD \\cdot CA.\n\\]\n\n3. Since \\( CT \\) is perpendicular to \\( AB \\), the angles \\( \\angle CTE \\) and \\( \\angle CDE \\) are both \\( 90^\\circ \\). This makes quadrilateral \\( CDTE \\) cyclic.\n\n4. Because \\( CDTE \\) is cyclic, we can use the Power of a Point theorem. For point \\( A \\), we have:\n\\[\nAE \\cdot AT = AD \\cdot AC.\n\\]\n\n5. To express \\( AD \\cdot AC \\) in another form, note that:\n\\[\nAD \\cdot AC = AC^2 - CD \\cdot CA,\n\\]\nusing the fact that \\( AD \\cdot AC = AC^2 - CD \\cdot CA \\).\n\n6. Substituting \\( CD \\cdot CA \\) with \\( CB^2 \\) (from the similarity of the triangles \\( CBD \\) and \\( CAB \\)), we get:\n\\[\nAE \\cdot AT = AC^2 - CB^2.\n\\]\n\n7. Furthermore, by the Pythagorean theorem in the right triangle \\( CTE \\) (and noting that \\( T \\) lies on segment \\( AB \\)):\n\\[\nAT^2 - BT^2 = AC^2 - CB^2,\n\\]\nwhere \\( AT \\) and \\( BT \\) represent the segments from \\( A \\) and \\( B \\) respectively to \\( T \\).\n\n8. Since the right-hand side of the equation does not change regardless of the position of \\( D \\), it shows that:\n\\[\nAE = \\text{constant}.\n\\]\n\n### Conclusion:\n\\[ E \\] is a fixed point. \n\\[\n\\boxed{E \\text{ is a fixed point.}}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Prove that for positive values of \\( a, b, \\) and \\( c \\) the following inequality holds:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\left(a^{2}+a+1\\right)\\left(b^{2}+b+1\\right)\\left(c^{2}+c+1\\right)}{a b c} \\geqslant 27\n$$", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, let's see. I need to prove that for positive a, b, c, the inequality [(a\u00b2 + a + 1)(b\u00b2 + b + 1)(c\u00b2 + c + 1)] / (abc) \u2265 27 holds. Hmm, so first, I should probably try to simplify the expression or find some way to apply known inequalities like AM-GM or Cauchy-Schwarz. Let me start by looking at each term in the numerator. Each factor is of the form (x\u00b2 + x + 1). Maybe I