, where I misinterpreted the effect of large a on the expression. In reality, when a is large, the numerator becomes enormous, leading to a very large ratio, which is indeed greater than 27.\n\nTherefore, the correct proof is as follows:\n\nFor each positive real number x, we have x\u00b2 +x +1 \u22653x by AM-GM inequality, since x\u00b2 +x +1 \u22653*(x\u00b2 *x *1)^{1/3}=3x. Multiplying this inequality for x=a, b, c gives (a\u00b2 +a +1)(b\u00b2 +b +1)(c\u00b2 +c +1) \u226527abc. Dividing both sides by abc yields the desired inequality. Equality holds when a=b=c=1.", "deepseek_solution": "To prove the inequality for positive \\( a, b, c \\):\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\left(a^{2}+a+1\\right)\\left(b^{2}+b+1\\right)\\left(c^{2}+c+1\\right)}{a b c} \\geqslant 27,\n$$\n\n**Step-by-Step Explanation:**\n\n1. **Simplify Each Factor:**\n   For each variable \\( x > 0 \\), observe that:\n   $$\n   \\frac{x^{2} + x + 1}{x} = x + 1 + \\frac{1}{x}.\n   $$\n   Thus, the original expression becomes:\n   $$\n   \\left(a + 1 + \\frac{1}{a}\\right)\\left(b + 1 + \\frac{1}{b}\\right)\\left(c + 1 + \\frac{1}{c}\\right).\n   $$\n\n2. **Apply the AM-GM Inequality:**\n   The Arithmetic Mean-Geometric Mean (AM-GM) inequality states that for non-negative numbers, the arithmetic mean is at least the geometric mean. For each term \\( x + 1 + \\frac{1}{x} \\), apply AM-GM to the three positive terms \\( x \\), \\( 1 \\), and \\( \\frac{1}{x} \\):\n   $$\n   \\frac{x + 1 + \\frac{1}{x}}{3} \\geq \\sqrt[3]{x \\cdot 1 \\cdot \\frac{1}{x}} = 1.\n   $$\n   Multiplying through by 3 gives:\n   $$\n   x + 1 + \\frac{1}{x} \\geq 3.\n   $$\n   Equality holds if and only if \\( x = 1 \\).\n\n3. **Multiply the Inequalities:**\n   Applying the above to \\( a \\), \\( b \\), and \\( c \\):\n   $$\n   \\left(a + 1 + \\frac{1}{a}\\right) \\geq 3, \\\\\n   \\left(b + 1 + \\frac{1}{b}\\right) \\geq 3, \\\\\n   \\left(c + 1 + \\frac{1}{c}\\right) \\geq 3.\n   $$\n   Multiplying these three inequalities together:\n   $$\n   \\left(a + 1 + \\frac{1}{a}\\right)\\left(b + 1 + \\frac{1}{b}\\right)\\left(c + 1 + \\frac{1}{c}\\right) \\geq 3 \\times 3 \\times 3 = 27.\n   $$\n\n4. **Conclusion:**\n   Therefore, substituting back into the original expression:\n   $$\n   \\frac{\\left(a^{2}+a+1\\right)\\left(b^{2}+b+1\\right)\\left(c^{2}+c+1\\right)}{a b c} \\geq 27.\n   $$\n   Equality occurs when \\( a = b = c = 1 \\).\n\n**Answer:**  \nThe inequality holds for all positive \\( a, b, c \\), with equality when \\( a = b = c = 1 \\). Thus,\n\n$$\n\\boxed{\\frac{\\left(a^{2}+a+1\\right)\\left(b^{2}+b+1\\right)\\left(c^{2}+c+1\\right)}{a b c} \\geqslant 27}\n$$", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. **Given Problem:**\n\n   Prove that for positive values \\(a, b,\\) and \\(c\\), the following inequality is true:\n   \n   \\[\n   \\frac{\\left(a^{2}+a+1\\right)\\left(b^{2}+b+1\\right)\\left(c^{2}+c+1\\right)}{abc} \\geq 27\n   \\]\n\n2. **First Step:** We need to prove that \\(\\frac{a^{2}+a+1}{a} \\geq 3\\), \\(\\frac{b^{2}+b+1}{b} \\geq 3\\), and \\(\\frac{c^{2}+c+1}{c} \\geq 3\\) separately.\n\n   - Consider the inequality \\(\\frac{a^{2}+a+1}{a} \\geq 3\\):\n     \\[\n     \\frac{a^{2}+a+1}{a} = a + 1 + \\frac{1}{a}\n     \\]\n     We want to show:\n     \\[\n     a + 1 + \\frac{1}{a} \\geq 3\n     \\]\n\n     This inequality can be deduced from AM-GM inequality which states that for two positive real numbers, the arithmetic mean is greater than or equal to the geometric mean:\n     \\[\n     a + \\frac{1}{a} \\geq 2\n     \\]\n\n     Thus:\n     \\[\n     a + 1 + \\frac{1}{a} \\geq a + 2\n     \\]\n\n     Clearly, since \\(a > 0\\):\n     \\[\n     a + 2 \\geq 3 \\implies a + 1 + \\frac{1}{a} \\geq 3\n     \\]\n\n     This proves the required inequality \\(\\frac{a^{2}+a+1}{a} \\geq 3\\).\n\n   - Similarly, we can show:\n     \\[\n     \\frac{b^{2}+b+1}{b} \\geq 3 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\frac{c^{2}+c+1}{c} \\geq 3\n     \\]\n\n3. **Second Step:** Prove the product form of the initial inequa